 "result": "This repo is \"Tessel\", a Flask-like framework. Here is the order of calls from the WSGI server to your view:\n\n1. `Tessel.__call__`, `src/tessel/app.py:1618`. It just delegates to `wsgi_entry`.\n2. `Tessel.wsgi_entry`, `src/tessel/app.py:1566`. This is the real WSGI app, kept separate so middleware can wrap it. It calls `request_context(environ)` and then `ctx.push()`.\n3. `Tessel.request_context`, `src/tessel/app.py:1501`. It calls `AppContext.from_environ`, which builds the request context from the environ.\n4. `AppContext.push`, `src/tessel/ctx.py:416`. It does these in order:\n   - Sets the context var.\n   - Sends the `appcontext_pushed` signal.\n   - Opens the session with `_get_session()`.\n   - Calls `match_request()` at `src/tessel/ctx.py:405`, which does URL routing. It stores `url_rule` and `view_args`, or a `routing_exception`.\n5. `Tessel.run_full_request`, `src/tessel/app.py:992`. It sends the `request_started` signal and then calls `preprocess_request`.\n6. `Tessel.preprocess_request`, `src/tessel/app.py:1366`. It runs the URL preprocessors and `before_request` functions. If one returns a value, the view is skipped.\n7. `Tessel.run_view`, `src/tessel/app.py:966`. It does these in order:\n   - Re-raises any routing exception.\n   - Handles automatic OPTIONS responses.\n   - Calls `self.ensure_sync(self.view_functions[rule.endpoint])(**view_args)` at line 990. `ensure_sync` is at `src/tessel/app.py:1065`.\n\nAfter the view returns, `finalize_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:1021`) turns the return value into a response. If anything above raises, `route_user_fault` handles it.",
